Here's a minor suggestion - I'll leave it to you whether to do it or not. It might not make any difference.

In the most common case, all three of these checks will give `false`. Is it worth optimizing for that common case by reducing it to one check in that case? For example:

```cs
(typeof(TResult).Assembly == thisAssembly) && (... existing checks ...)
````

I guess we could even 100% block any confusion about using the concrete implementation types (`InvokeAsync<JSObjectReference>`) like:

```cs
if (typeof(TResult).Assembly == thisAssembly)
{
    if (it is any of the three accepted interface types)
    {
        return JSCallResultType.JSObjectReference;
    }
    else
    {
        throw InvalidArgumentException("JS interop cannot supply an instance of type {TResult}");
    }
}
else
{
    return JSCallResultType.Default;
}

```

where `thisAssembly` is a static readonly.
